1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,371
Your credit card company made money from you last

2
00:00:02,371 --> 00:00:04,692
month. Even if you never paid a cent of

3
00:00:04,692 --> 00:00:04,950
interest.

4
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:07,592
And the fourth way they do it is so

5
00:00:07,592 --> 00:00:09,220
sneaky… most people never notice it.

6
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:11,510
Stick around, because at the end I'll show you

7
00:00:11,510 --> 00:00:13,600
how to flip the game… and make them lose.

8
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:15,330
First, look at this number.

9
00:00:15,530 --> 00:00:18,446
One point two nine trillion dollars. That's what Americans

10
00:00:18,446 --> 00:00:20,390
owe on credit cards right now.

11
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:22,940
A record. And it keeps growing.

12
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:25,868
And the average interest rate on cards that carry

13
00:00:25,868 --> 00:00:27,990
a balance? Around twenty-two percent a year.

14
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:31,356
Twenty-two percent. Keep that number in your head. It's

15
00:00:31,356 --> 00:00:33,770
going to come back… and it's not pretty.

16
00:00:34,270 --> 00:00:36,498
But before we get into it… how did we

17
00:00:36,498 --> 00:00:38,725
end up with cards in every wallet? The story

18
00:00:38,725 --> 00:00:39,220
is wild.

19
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:43,095
New York, nineteen fifty. A businessman named Frank McNamara

20
00:00:43,095 --> 00:00:46,470
goes out to dinner… and, according to the famous

21
00:00:46,470 --> 00:00:48,720
story, realizes he forgot his wallet.

22
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:51,540
Out of that idea came Diners Club, a card

23
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:54,161
you could use at restaurants and pay later. Many

24
00:00:54,161 --> 00:00:56,490
people call it the first modern credit card.

25
00:00:56,690 --> 00:01:00,040
Then, in nineteen fifty-eight, Bank of America did something

26
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:03,390
nobody would dare today. It mailed sixty thousand credit

27
00:01:03,390 --> 00:01:06,740
cards to people in Fresno, California. Without them asking.

28
00:01:07,390 --> 00:01:09,980
That card later became… Visa.

29
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:13,127
And today, credit cards are one of the most

30
00:01:13,127 --> 00:01:15,420
profitable products in banking. Here's exactly how.

31
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:18,140
There are four ways they make money. Let's go.

32
00:01:19,090 --> 00:01:20,830
Way number one. Interest.

33
00:01:21,330 --> 00:01:22,490
Here's a simple example.

34
00:01:22,690 --> 00:01:25,590
You owe five thousand dollars. At twenty-two percent.

35
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:28,677
And you only pay the minimum each month. Like

36
00:01:28,677 --> 00:01:29,760
millions of people do.

37
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:31,490
Guess how long it takes to pay it off.

38
00:01:31,690 --> 00:01:32,840
About nineteen years.

39
00:01:33,490 --> 00:01:36,391
And the interest? More than eight thousand dollars. More

40
00:01:36,391 --> 00:01:37,680
than what you borrowed.

41
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:40,421
Now watch what happens if you pay two hundred

42
00:01:40,421 --> 00:01:41,350
dollars a month instead.

43
00:01:41,550 --> 00:01:45,032
Done in under three years. Around eighteen hundred in

44
00:01:45,032 --> 00:01:47,740
interest. Same debt. Six thousand dollars saved.

45
00:01:48,390 --> 00:01:50,764
That's why the minimum payment looks so friendly. It's

46
00:01:50,764 --> 00:01:53,139
not there to help you. It's there to keep

47
00:01:53,139 --> 00:01:53,930
the balance alive.

48
00:01:54,130 --> 00:01:57,010
Oh, and one more interest trap. Watch out for

49
00:01:57,010 --> 00:01:59,890
deferred interest deals. No interest if paid in full

50
00:01:59,890 --> 00:02:00,850
in twelve months.

51
00:02:01,050 --> 00:02:03,547
Sounds great. But if you still owe even a

52
00:02:03,547 --> 00:02:06,043
little on the last day, many of these deals

53
00:02:06,043 --> 00:02:08,540
charge you all the interest from day one. Retroactively.

54
00:02:09,190 --> 00:02:12,528
The government's own consumer finance agency warns about this.

55
00:02:12,528 --> 00:02:15,865
Always read whether it says deferred interest… or zero

56
00:02:15,865 --> 00:02:18,090
percent APR. They're not the same.

57
00:02:18,740 --> 00:02:21,220
But interest isn't even their only trick. Because even

58
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:23,700
if you pay in full… they still get paid.

59
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:26,270
Way number two. Swipe fees.

60
00:02:26,770 --> 00:02:29,324
Every time you tap your card, the store pays

61
00:02:29,324 --> 00:02:31,310
a fee. Usually a couple of percent.

62
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:34,930
A couple of percent sounds tiny. Now multiply it

63
00:02:34,930 --> 00:02:36,580
by every purchase in America.

64
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:40,151
One hundred ninety-eight billion dollars. In fees. In just

65
00:02:40,151 --> 00:02:40,900
one year.

66
00:02:41,550 --> 00:02:44,323
So who really pays it? Stores raise prices to

67
00:02:44,323 --> 00:02:47,096
cover it. Which means… you do. Even if you

68
00:02:47,096 --> 00:02:48,020
pay with cash.

69
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:51,393
A Boston Fed study estimated each cash-paying household was

70
00:02:51,393 --> 00:02:54,565
paying about one hundred forty-nine dollars a year… to

71
00:02:54,565 --> 00:02:56,680
help fund other people's card rewards.

72
00:02:57,330 --> 00:02:59,980
Yep. Your free cashback isn't really free.

73
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:02,859
Quick myth busting while we're here. Many people think

74
00:03:02,859 --> 00:03:05,538
carrying a balance helps your credit score. It doesn't.

75
00:03:05,538 --> 00:03:08,217
Paying in full is just as good for your

76
00:03:08,217 --> 00:03:09,110
score… and free.

77
00:03:09,310 --> 00:03:11,932
What actually matters most for your FICO score? Paying

78
00:03:11,932 --> 00:03:14,553
on time is the biggest factor. Then how much

79
00:03:14,553 --> 00:03:16,010
of your limit you use.

80
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:19,364
Way number three is the one that hurts the

81
00:03:19,364 --> 00:03:20,700
most when it hits. Fees.

82
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:26,150
Late fees. Annual fees. Cash advance fees. Foreign transaction

83
00:03:26,150 --> 00:03:28,350
fees. Balance transfer fees.

84
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,922
The worst one? The cash advance. A fee upfront,

85
00:03:31,922 --> 00:03:34,520
a higher rate, and interest from day one.

86
00:03:35,170 --> 00:03:37,414
If you remember one thing from this part: never

87
00:03:37,414 --> 00:03:39,160
use your credit card at an ATM.

88
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:41,661
And here's a question that tells you who really

89
00:03:41,661 --> 00:03:43,961
pays for the rewards. Who do you think the

90
00:03:43,961 --> 00:03:46,262
banks love more? People who pay in full… or

91
00:03:46,262 --> 00:03:47,540
people who carry a balance?

92
00:03:48,190 --> 00:03:51,077
Exactly. In the industry, people who carry a balance

93
00:03:51,077 --> 00:03:53,964
are often the most profitable customers. People who pay

94
00:03:53,964 --> 00:03:56,530
in full get the perks almost for free.

95
00:03:57,180 --> 00:03:59,962
Okay. Now the sneaky one. The one I promised

96
00:03:59,962 --> 00:04:00,890
at the beginning.

97
00:04:01,390 --> 00:04:03,420
Way number four. Your own brain.

98
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:07,737
Researchers at MIT ran an experiment. People bid on

99
00:04:07,737 --> 00:04:09,010
sold-out basketball tickets.

100
00:04:09,210 --> 00:04:11,511
Half of them had to pay cash. The other

101
00:04:11,511 --> 00:04:12,790
half, with a credit card.

102
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:16,082
The card group was willing to pay way more.

103
00:04:16,082 --> 00:04:17,550
In some cases, about double.

104
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:21,359
Same tickets. Same people. Just a different way to

105
00:04:21,359 --> 00:04:21,710
pay.

106
00:04:21,910 --> 00:04:25,310
It's called the pain of paying. Cash hurts. A

107
00:04:25,310 --> 00:04:28,710
card barely registers. And tapping your phone? Even less.

108
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:31,978
Add rewards on top, and spending starts to feel

109
00:04:31,978 --> 00:04:32,560
like winning.

110
00:04:33,060 --> 00:04:36,496
But wait. What about rewards? Points, miles, cashback. Aren't

111
00:04:36,496 --> 00:04:39,931
those free money? Let's do the math. Pause and

112
00:04:39,931 --> 00:04:41,840
guess before I show you.

113
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:44,794
Say you spend fifteen hundred dollars a month on

114
00:04:44,794 --> 00:04:46,430
a card with two percent cashback.

115
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:50,302
That's three hundred sixty dollars a year in rewards.

116
00:04:50,302 --> 00:04:50,660
Nice.

117
00:04:51,310 --> 00:04:54,284
Now imagine you carry that fifteen hundred as a

118
00:04:54,284 --> 00:04:57,259
balance at twenty-two percent. That's about twenty-eight dollars of

119
00:04:57,259 --> 00:04:58,250
interest… every month.

120
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:01,875
Carry it all year, and you pay around three

121
00:05:01,875 --> 00:05:04,849
hundred thirty-five dollars in interest. Almost all your rewards…

122
00:05:04,849 --> 00:05:05,180
gone.

123
00:05:05,830 --> 00:05:08,060
And that's with just one month of spending as

124
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:10,289
a balance. Carry more, and you're paying the bank

125
00:05:10,289 --> 00:05:11,280
to give you points.

126
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:13,973
So rewards are only free money for one type

127
00:05:13,973 --> 00:05:16,466
of person: the one who pays in full every

128
00:05:16,466 --> 00:05:17,020
single month.

129
00:05:17,220 --> 00:05:20,020
Another quick trap: your statement balance versus your current

130
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:22,819
balance. To avoid interest, you need to pay the

131
00:05:22,819 --> 00:05:25,619
statement balance by the due date. Not just the

132
00:05:25,619 --> 00:05:25,930
minimum.

133
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:28,822
And if a payment is thirty days late, it

134
00:05:28,822 --> 00:05:31,064
can be reported to the credit bureaus. That can

135
00:05:31,064 --> 00:05:32,310
hit your score for years.

136
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:36,108
Quick question before the dark side. Which card should

137
00:05:36,108 --> 00:05:38,640
you actually have? There are three main types.

138
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:42,096
Cashback cards. Simple. A small percent back on what

139
00:05:42,096 --> 00:05:45,052
you spend. Best for most people who pay in

140
00:05:45,052 --> 00:05:45,380
full.

141
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:48,464
Travel cards. Points and miles, often with an annual

142
00:05:48,464 --> 00:05:51,348
fee. Only worth it if you travel a lot

143
00:05:51,348 --> 00:05:52,950
and actually use the perks.

144
00:05:53,150 --> 00:05:56,075
And secured cards. You put down a deposit, and

145
00:05:56,075 --> 00:05:59,000
that becomes your limit. They're made to build or

146
00:05:59,000 --> 00:05:59,650
rebuild credit.

147
00:05:59,850 --> 00:06:02,040
And the best card of all? The one you

148
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:04,230
pay in full every month. No matter the logo.

149
00:06:04,730 --> 00:06:07,562
And what happens if you just… stop paying? A

150
00:06:07,562 --> 00:06:10,080
lot of people wonder this. Here's the timeline.

151
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:13,868
First, late fees. And on many cards, a penalty

152
00:06:13,868 --> 00:06:16,790
rate kicks in. Often close to thirty percent.

153
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:19,896
After about thirty days, the late payment can show

154
00:06:19,896 --> 00:06:21,260
up on your credit report.

155
00:06:21,460 --> 00:06:24,336
After around six months of no payments, banks usually

156
00:06:24,336 --> 00:06:27,213
charge off the debt. That doesn't mean it's forgiven.

157
00:06:27,213 --> 00:06:29,450
It's often sold or sent to collections.

158
00:06:29,650 --> 00:06:32,163
And negative marks like that can stay on your

159
00:06:32,163 --> 00:06:34,677
credit report for up to seven years. Making it

160
00:06:34,677 --> 00:06:37,190
harder to rent, borrow or even get some jobs.

161
00:06:37,840 --> 00:06:40,638
So if you're struggling, the best move is to

162
00:06:40,638 --> 00:06:43,436
call the bank early. Many offer hardship plans. Waiting

163
00:06:43,436 --> 00:06:44,680
only makes it worse.

164
00:06:44,880 --> 00:06:48,005
Okay. You've seen all their tricks. Now let's turn

165
00:06:48,005 --> 00:06:48,700
the tables.

166
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:51,145
So now you know how they win. Here's how

167
00:06:51,145 --> 00:06:52,010
you make them lose.

168
00:06:52,510 --> 00:06:55,950
Rule one. Pay the full statement balance. Every month.

169
00:06:56,150 --> 00:06:59,255
On most cards, that means zero interest on purchases.

170
00:06:59,255 --> 00:07:00,980
Thanks to the grace period.

171
00:07:01,630 --> 00:07:04,189
Rule two. Turn on autopay, at least for the

172
00:07:04,189 --> 00:07:06,748
minimum. One late payment can cost you a fee

173
00:07:06,748 --> 00:07:08,170
and hurt your credit score.

174
00:07:08,370 --> 00:07:11,998
Rule three. Got debt? Attack the highest interest rate

175
00:07:11,998 --> 00:07:14,820
first. Mathematically, that saves you the most.

176
00:07:15,470 --> 00:07:18,247
Rule four. Call and ask for a lower rate.

177
00:07:18,247 --> 00:07:19,790
It costs nothing to ask.

178
00:07:19,990 --> 00:07:22,989
Bonus for rule three: a zero percent balance transfer

179
00:07:22,989 --> 00:07:25,988
can buy you time. Just know there's usually a

180
00:07:25,988 --> 00:07:28,987
transfer fee, often three to five percent, and a

181
00:07:28,987 --> 00:07:29,320
deadline.

182
00:07:29,520 --> 00:07:32,525
Rule five, part one: keep your card balance well

183
00:07:32,525 --> 00:07:35,529
below your limit. Many experts suggest staying under thirty

184
00:07:35,529 --> 00:07:38,200
percent. Lower is even better for your score.

185
00:07:38,850 --> 00:07:41,745
And rule five. Decide the amount before you shop.

186
00:07:41,745 --> 00:07:44,640
That brings back the pain of paying… on purpose.

187
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:47,249
Do this, and the bank pays you rewards… while

188
00:07:47,249 --> 00:07:48,320
you pay them nothing.

189
00:07:48,970 --> 00:07:51,433
So tell me: do you pay your card in

190
00:07:51,433 --> 00:07:53,897
full every month? Be honest. And subscribe, because next

191
00:07:53,897 --> 00:07:56,360
I'll show you why the casino always wins… and

192
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:58,550
the trick they use to make you stay.
